WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.542
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE:
Serverless

00:00:01.542 --> 00:00:03.000
is much more than a buzzword.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.290
And in this episode
of "GCP Essentials,"

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:07.780
you'll understand how
Google Cloud Platform

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:09.837
makes serverless work for you.

00:00:09.837 --> 00:00:12.172
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.650
If you look back, most of the
early days of Google Cloud

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:21.950
focused on what could be called
a serverless offering, as we've

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:24.830
implemented and offered
products with this fundamental

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:26.000
principle.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.380
You give us your code.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.000
We do the rest.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.790
There are no servers to manage
and no cluster to set up,

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:33.740
provision, or upgrade.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:36.650
In addition, the
infrastructure autoscales

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:39.380
to meet the load demand,
and you are only billed

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.630
for the resources consumed.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:44.630
As a developer building
web apps, mobile APIs,

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:47.030
or back-end services,
this is your ability

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:50.300
to focus on the business
logic of your apps.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.490
GCP offers a number
of different products

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:54.990
to help you accomplish
exactly what you need.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.060
Let's take a look.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.400
Functions as a Service, or
FaaS, is a very popular approach

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.853
to serverless compute.

00:01:00.853 --> 00:01:02.270
Developers write
a piece of code--

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:04.940
a function-- that is
triggered on an event

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.220
or on an incoming HTTP request.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.910
This function code can talk to
a database and to other services

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.160
before it generates
itself another event

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.140
or simply sends a response back.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:18.890
Google Cloud Functions supports
multiple programming languages,

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:24.650
such as Python, Node.js,
Go, Java, and more to come,

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:27.530
and these can be used with
a large set of libraries

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:29.090
and frameworks.

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:31.007
Simply deploy your
function along

00:01:31.007 --> 00:01:33.590
with its list of dependencies,
which will be resolved directly

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.750
in the cloud, and
configure the event that

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:37.550
will trigger its execution.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:40.080
This can be an HTTP
event, of course,

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.320
but also a file upload to
a Cloud Storage bucket,

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.540
data changing in a
database, a message

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:47.120
being posted to a
pub/sub queue or topic,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.980
or several other events.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:52.050
Functions can be assigned deploy
time environment variables.

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.710
They can be deployed to
any and multiple regions

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:57.230
from a single project,
and they support up

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:00.470
to several gigabytes of memory.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:03.320
Functions can also be configured
with specific security

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.930
constraints, such as
least privilege access

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:08.479
with per function identity.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:11.540
You can even use the Compute
metadata server to fetch

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:14.250
identity tokens at runtime.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.730
Cloud Functions are billed
by the number of invocations,

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:19.790
the compute time, and the
outgoing network, or egress,

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.790
with free first 2 million
invocations every month.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:24.740
Cloud Functions are
probably the easiest way

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:27.590
to access one of the many
powerful GCP services,

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:30.650
from machine learning APIs to
other storage and processing

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.500
solutions that have been used
by customers to implement

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.300
everything from
glue code to fully

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.910
fledged microservices-based
applications.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:40.790
Now, as a developer,
you may want

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:43.670
even more freedom in the
languages and frameworks

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:44.730
that you use.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.640
And most importantly,
you may rather

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.880
want to hand over a carefully
crafted Docker container

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.160
image instead of source code.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.640
Cloud Run is here to offer you
a true serverless experience,

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.690
if a stateless HTTP
container image is

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:01.310
what you'd like to use.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:04.400
Simply build the image,
upload it to Cloud Registry,

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:08.090
and create a Cloud Run
service using that container.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.998
Your app is now deployed
and running in the cloud.

00:03:10.998 --> 00:03:13.040
And you can forget about
provisioning or managing

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:13.790
servers.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.910
Cloud Run does that for you.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.860
It automatically and
quickly scales up and down

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:22.820
based on your incoming traffic,
and even scales down to zero.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:26.090
You pay only for the resources
that your app uses down

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:28.880
to the nearest
100th millisecond.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.940
Because Cloud Run is
built with Knative,

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:36.170
you can use it with your own
Kubernetes Engine cluster, too.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:39.350
With Cloud Run on GKE,
you can build and deploy

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.810
apps to your own
Kubernetes cluster

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:45.100
with the same easy
experience and benefits.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:48.320
Whether running on
GKE or not, Cloud Run

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:51.350
supports deploying multiple
services in a single GCP

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:54.140
project, either in
multiple regions

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:58.790
or in specific namespaces
when running in a GKE cluster.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.700
Each service exposes
a unique endpoint.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.370
Each service deployment
creates a revision,

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:07.010
and requests are
automatically routed as soon

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.460
as possible to the latest
healthy service revision.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:13.760
Finally, each revision
receiving requests

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:15.830
is automatically
scaled to the number

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:20.959
of container instances needed
to handle all these requests.

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:22.880
Note that a given
container instance

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.550
can receive multiple
requests at the same time.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:27.170
With the concurrency
setting, you

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:29.540
can set the maximum
number of requests

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:32.660
that can be sent in parallel
to a given container instance.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:36.290
Cloud Run essentially
gives you the flexibility

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:38.960
of modern container-based
development

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.350
with all the benefits of a
fully serverless environment,

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:45.510
autoscaling to your
application's needs.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:47.780
If you are building an
entire web application

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.970
with multiple services
and would still

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:53.480
like to deploy source code while
preserving all the serverless

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:57.470
benefits, you should check
out Google App Engine.

00:04:57.470 --> 00:04:59.780
App Engine has
recently celebrated

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:02.300
its 10th anniversary,
and is where

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:06.530
you'll find Google Cloud
Platform's serverless routes.

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:08.510
App Engine is a
managed platform that

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:11.660
lets you pick a language
you're most comfortable with.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:15.670
Simply run gcloud app
deploy, and App Engine

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.490
will take care of
uploading your code

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:20.050
and running it on
Google's infrastructure.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:24.240
The second-generation App Engine
runtimes, available for Java,

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:30.330
Node, PHP, Go, and Python, offer
a truly idiomatic experience

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:34.440
with the ability to use any
language API and any framework

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.250
with read/write file system
access and with isolation

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:40.140
provided by gVisor--

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:44.670
a powerful open-source
sandbox technology.

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.710
App Engine applications
can be built

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:49.470
using multiple
services, each of which

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:54.060
can use different languages and
can be scaled independently.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.430
Each service can offer
multiple versions,

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:59.520
all active at the same time,
and it is trivial for you

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.930
to set up staged rollouts or A/B
testing across these versions

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.360
with traffic splitting.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.750
App Engine takes out-of-the-box
tooling one step further with

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:13.120
full app performance
management tools available,

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.540
such as live debugging
of production apps,

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.600
tracing of requests
flowing across your system,

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:22.320
and even CPU and heap
profiling of your app.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:25.440
Google App Engine is a mature
serverless platform and not

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:27.570
your father's
platform as a service.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:29.550
Modern and more
advanced applications

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:32.490
can be written with a
combination of Cloud Functions,

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:35.070
App Engine, and Cloud Run.

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.920
And both Cloud Pub/Sub
and Cloud Tasks

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.690
are popular and
elegant solutions

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.580
for integrating these
different parts,

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:44.850
or even simply to combine
multiple functions.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:48.540
Cloud Pub/Sub is a simple,
reliable, and scalable event

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:51.150
system, offering
many-to-many asynchronous

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:55.170
messaging that nicely decouples
senders and receivers.

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:57.360
It supports
at-least-once delivery

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:00.420
and is global by default with
no infrastructure to manage.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:03.450
Just publish and consume
up to hundreds of millions

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:05.820
of messages per second.

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:09.750
Cloud Tasks, on the other
hand, offers a dispatch system

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:13.440
to manage the execution of large
numbers of distributed tasks.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:16.860
It is ideal for one-to-one
asynchronous messaging

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:19.320
and comes with rate
limit controls.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:23.940
Finally, Cloud Scheduler is a
fully managed cron job service,

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:25.950
enabling you to
schedule tasks that

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:29.040
are invoked through HTTPS
endpoints, Cloud Pub/Sub

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:31.500
topics, or App
Engine applications.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.710
It's remarkably simple and yet
incredibly powerful to use.

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:36.390
Check it out.

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:38.880
All of these solutions
briefly discussed

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.940
here are fully
managed and monitored.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:46.300
Logging and error reporting
are just built in.

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:50.770
With GCP serverless, you give us
your code in whichever artifact

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:51.700
you prefer--

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:55.390
a function, an application,
or a container image--

00:07:55.390 --> 00:07:58.330
and we'll run it for you.

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:01.120
Consider taking free codelabs
linked in the description

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:05.230
below to explore these products,
and look forward to upcoming

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:08.080
episodes and more overviews.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:11.110
If you liked this video, please
like, subscribe, comment,

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:12.160
and share.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.500
See you soon for another
"GCP Essentials" video.

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:17.250
